Richard E. Hawkins wrote:

> It predates repetitive stress notions, so that wouldn't be the issue.
> But give your fingers a wiggle:  not only is it significantly weaker, it
> has significantly less mobility than the index finger.  HOwever, this
> wouldn't likely have been an issue in writing an editor at the time.

I don't really see finger strength as an issue on modern keyboards, and
mobility doesn't seem to be an issue either if the key in question is right
under the finger to begin with.

> The diagonals are much later than hjkl.  However, I don't "move my index
> finger to type 'h'", I simply type an h.  Come to think of it, these
> days I don't even type an h; I just move left . . .

Well, quite; it seems to me that the whole point of clustering the keys in
this way is that you don't have to translate "left" to 'h' each time you
want to move until the pattern is permanently engraved in your cerebellum.
Learning the movements is closer to a natural key pattern than memorizing a
direction to character mapping.

>> I have no idea what you mean by this.  In any case, I'm not particularly
>> interested in the historical reasons for the choice unless they still
>> apply today.
>
> But this whole thing was about why that combination . . .

Sure, but for me "historical reasons combined with inertia" is
sufficient if those historical reasons are not valid these days.

Anyway, I think we're getting to dead horse territory here, and I don't use
the vi movement keys.  Perhaps I'll give them a go one of these days; the
number pad is also unnatural and inconvenient for me in some ways.

